<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S4.T1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Accuracy and efficiency comparison of HIVTP with prior methods on LLaVA-v1.5-7B under different upper bounds of the visual token retaining ratio, <math id="S4.T1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\text{max}}\in\{75\%,50\%,40\%,25\%\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>75</mn><mo mathsize="1.110em">%</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo mathsize="1.110em">%</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>40</mn><mo mathsize="1.110em">%</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>25</mn><mo mathsize="1.110em">%</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\text{max}}\in\{75\%,50\%,40\%,25\%\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The actual retaining ratio is denoted by <math id="S4.T1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\text{retain}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>retain</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\text{retain}}</annotation></semantics></math>. TTFT and Throughput are computed as averages across all benchmarks. The best results among all methods for each <math id="S4.T1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\text{max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\text{max}}</annotation></semantics></math> are highlighted in <span id="S4.T1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bold</span>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experimental Results</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we systematically evaluate our proposed HIVTP on eight different benchmarks when applied to LLaVA-v1.5-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> and LLaVA-Next-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, and compare it with prior methods: FoPru <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, VisionZip <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>, PruMerge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>, and HiRED <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We adopt eight widely used benchmarks, including: 1) MME <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, for evaluating visual perception and recognition abilities; 2) POPE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, for assessing the model’s ability to avoid object hallucination; 3) TextVQA (<math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{VQA}^{\text{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>VQA</mi><mtext>T</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{VQA}^{\text{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>)  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>, for evaluating visual reasoning ability based on textual content in images; 4) MMBench (MMB) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, for evaluating multimodal understanding and reasoning capabilities; 5) ScienceQA (SQA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>, for evaluating the ability to answer scientific questions across multiple domains using image content; 6) OCRBench (OCR) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, for recognizing and reasoning about text within images; 7) GQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, for evaluating the ability to answer questions about real-world scene graphs. and 8) Ai2D <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, for evaluating the ability to understand and answer questions about scientific diagrams.
We conduct experiments under different upper bounds of the visual token keeping ratio, <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\text{max}}\in\{75\%,50\%,40\%,25\%\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>75</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>40</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>25</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\text{max}}\in\{75\%,50\%,40\%,25\%\}</annotation></semantics></math>. For LLaVA-Next, the original input image is divided into several parts as sub-images. Each sub-image, as well as the original image itself, is independently processed by the vision encoder to obtain its own visual tokens. We apply HIVTP to the visual tokens from both the original image and all sub-images. For prior methods, we set their actual visual token keeping ratio <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\text{keep}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>keep</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\text{keep}}</annotation></semantics></math> to match <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\text{max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\text{max}}</annotation></semantics></math>. All experiments are conducted using an NVIDIA A40 GPU.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Middle Layers Setting.</span> To determine the index set of middle layers <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> used for computing visual token importance scores in our method, we visualize the attention heatmaps at different layers of the vision encoder in LLaVA-v1.5-7B for several examples from the MME benchmark. One representative visualization is shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.3 Hierarchical Visual Token Pruning ‣ 4 HIVTP ‣ HIVTP: A Training-Free Method to Improve VLMs Efficiency via Hierarchical Visual Token Pruning Using Middle-Layer-Based Importance Score" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, and additional examples are provided in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Visualization of Attention Heatmap ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ HIVTP: A Training-Free Method to Improve VLMs Efficiency via Hierarchical Visual Token Pruning Using Middle-Layer-Based Importance Score" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>.
As observed, from layer 7 to layer 10, the visual tokens corresponding to the main objects in the image exhibit higher brightness, indicating that they receive higher attention. Based on this observation, We select layers 7 to layer 10 as the middle layers.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S5.T2.3" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:486.9pt;height:94pt;vertical-align:-46.6pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-790.0pt,152.4pt) scale(0.235587879821285,0.235587879821285) ;">
<table id="S5.T2.3.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S5.T2.3.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T2.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" style="padding:-0.25pt 2.0pt;"></td>
<td id="S5.T2.3.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" style="padding:-0.25pt 2.0pt;"></td>
<td id="S5.T2.3.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" style="padding:-0.25pt 2.0pt;"></td>
<td id="S5.T2.3.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" style="padding:-0.25pt 2.0pt;" colspan="8"><span id="S5.T2.3.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">Accuracy Performance</span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.3.1.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding:-0.25pt 2.0pt;" colspan="2"><span id="S5.T2.3.1.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">Efficiency</span></td></tr>
